Document (dev.stxt.website): STXT vs TOML: flat configuration vs hierarchical documents
	Metadata:
		Last modif: 2026-09-19
		Description: STXT and TOML compared with examples: headers versus indentation, nested tables, multi-line strings, types in the syntax versus types in the definition, and validation.

	Header: @STXT@ vs TOML

	Assert >>
		TOML and @STXT@ share a goal: files that a person writes and reads.
		TOML describes tables of keys with typed values, and @STXT@ a tree of text nodes.

	Subheader: The same content, twice

	Content >>
		The configuration of a backup in TOML:

	Listing >>
		title = "Nightly backup"

		[storage]
		kind = "s3"
		bucket = "acme-backups"

		[[job]]
		name = "database"
		schedule = "02:30"
		keep = 14

		[[job]]
		name = "documents"
		schedule = "03:00"
		keep = 30

	Content >>
		And in @STXT@:

	Code >>
		Backup: Nightly backup
			Storage:
				Kind: s3
				Bucket: acme-backups
			Job: database
				Schedule: 02:30
				Keep: 14
			Job: documents
				Schedule: 03:00
				Keep: 30

	Content >>
		The tree is the same. The differences are these:

		|                 | TOML                                                  | @STXT@                                                    |
		| --------------- | ----------------------------------------------------- | --------------------------------------------------------- |
		| Hierarchy       | In the `[storage]` and `[[job]]` headers              | In the indentation                                        |
		| Spellings       | Headers, dotted keys, inline tables, arrays of tables | A single one                                              |
		| Values          | Typed by their spelling: `14`, `"14"`, `true`         | Every value is text. The type is declared by the template |
		| Multi-line text | `"""` strings, not indented                           | A block node `>>`, indented with the document             |
		| Namespaces      | None                                                  | Part of the language                                      |
		| Schemas         | External tools                                        | Part of the language, and optional                        |

	Subheader: Where the hierarchy lives

	Content >>
		Every TOML header writes the full path of its table. Two jobs with their steps:

	Listing >>
		[[job]]
		name = "database"

		[[job.step]]
		run = "pg_dump acme > acme.sql"

		[[job.step]]
		run = "gzip acme.sql"

		[[job]]
		name = "documents"

		[[job.step]]
		run = "tar cf documents.tar /srv/docs"

	Content >>
		Each `[[job.step]]` belongs to the last `[[job]]` opened. Membership is decided by
		position in the file, and the path is repeated in every header.

		In @STXT@ every level is written once, and a child belongs to the node it is
		indented under ([STXT-SPEC §8](stxt-core-ref#s8)):

	Code >>
		Backup:
			Job: database
				Step: pg_dump acme > acme.sql
				Step: gzip acme.sql
			Job: documents
				Step: tar cf documents.tar /srv/docs

	Content >>
		In addition, the same structure can be written in TOML in three ways:

	Listing >>
		# 1. With a header
		[storage]
		kind = "s3"

		# 2. With a dotted key
		storage.kind = "s3"

		# 3. With an inline table
		storage = { kind = "s3" }

	Content >>
		Tables can also go in any order, so the visual shape of the file
		may not match the tree. In @STXT@ the visual shape of the document is its structure.

	Subheader: Multi-line text

	Content >>
		A TOML multi-line string keeps the indentation of each line.
		For the value not to contain it, the text cannot be indented along with the table it belongs to:

	Listing >>
		[storage]
		kind = "s3"
		notes = """
		Restarted after the 2026-08-31 incident.
		The TLS certificate expires on 2027-03-01.
		"""

	Content >>
		In an @STXT@ block node the indentation of the level does not count, and only the additional
		indentation is part of the text ([STXT-SPEC §10](stxt-core-ref#s10)):

	Code >>
		Storage:
			Kind: s3
			Notes >>
				Restarted after the 2026-08-31 incident.
				The TLS certificate expires on 2027-03-01.

	Subheader: Types

	Content >>
		In TOML the type is in the spelling of the value:

		| Value                  | Type      |
		| ---------------------- | --------- |
		| `14`                   | Integer   |
		| `"14"`                 | String    |
		| `true`                 | Boolean   |
		| `2026-08-31T02:30:00Z` | Date-time |

		A TOML parser returns typed data, with no external definition. In return, strings
		always go in quotes, in four forms (basic and literal, single-line and multi-line),
		with escape characters in the basic ones.

		In @STXT@ a value is always text, without quotes. Types (`NATURAL`, `DATE`, `TIMESTAMP`, `EMAIL`...)
		are declared in the template and checked by the validator. Without a template, `14` is two characters.

	Subheader: Namespaces and validation

	Content >>
		In TOML nothing declares which vocabulary the file belongs to, and validation is left to the application
		or to an external tool. In @STXT@ the namespace connects the document with its template.
		The configuration from the beginning, with a namespace:

	Code >>
		Backup (com.example.backup): Nightly backup
			Storage:
				Kind: s3
				Bucket: acme-backups
			Job: database
				Schedule: 02:30
				Keep: 14
			Job: documents
				Schedule: 03:00
				Keep: 30

	Content >>
		And the template for its namespace:

	Code >>
		Template (@stxt.template): com.example.backup
			Structure >>
				Backup (com.example.backup):
					Storage: (1)
						Kind: (1)
						Bucket: (1)
					Job: (+)
						Schedule: (1)
						Keep: (1) NATURAL

	Content >>
		A document with errors does not validate:

	Code >>
		# ERROR: this document does not validate
		Backup (com.example.backup): Nightly backup
			# `Storage` is missing
			Job: database
				Schedule: 02:30
				# `fourteen` is not a NATURAL
				Keep: fourteen
				# `Compress` is not in the template
				Compress: true

	Subheader: When to use each

	Content >>
		* **TOML**: where the ecosystem expects it (`Cargo.toml`, `pyproject.toml`), and for flat configuration
		  of keys with typed values, which is the goal its specification declares.
		* **@STXT@**: when the file grows into a document. Several levels of hierarchy, prose
		  next to the data, or a structure that must be validated. For example,
		  [configuration files](use-cases-config-files) or [corporate documents](use-cases-corporate-docs).

		The short version of this comparison is in the [FAQ](faq#why-not-xml-yaml-or-toml).
